| Concern | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| **Encryption at rest** | AES-256-GCM; key derived from the master password via PBKDF2-SHA-256 (100 000 iterations) with a random 128-bit salt stored in the KV backend. |
| **Owner / name isolation** | Each secret is stored under a storage key that is `SHA-256("sec" + owner + \0 + name)`. The `(owner, name)` pair is also passed as GCM **additional authenticated data (AAD)**, so ciphertext from one secret cannot be replayed as another—even if an attacker copies the blob. |
| **Tamper detection** | GCM's 128-bit authentication tag covers both the ciphertext *and* the AAD. Any bit-flip in the stored value, or a mismatch in the AAD (wrong owner/name, wrong master password), causes `crypto.subtle.decrypt` to throw, and `get` re-throws as a generic `Error`. |
| **Wrong master password** | A different password derives a different AES key; every `get` on an existing secret will fail GCM authentication and throw. `createSecretStore` itself does not reject—it simply produces a store whose `get` calls throw. |
| **Index / listing** | A per-owner JSON array of names is kept under `SHA-256("idx" + owner)`. It is best-effort: corruption silently degrades `list` to `[]`, but `get`/`remove` rely on the per-secret hash key and are unaffected. |
| **No imports** | Only the global `crypto.subtle`, `crypto.getRandomValues`, `TextEncoder`, `TextDecoder`, and `JSON` are used. |

